          "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Text 2 opens by affirming the core of Morrison's case: Gates \"accepts that the dominant tradition cannot be cleansed by the addition of new names alone and that its central texts demand the kind of rereading she describes.\" His move is therefore not to contest her rereading project but to extend the critical agenda alongside it. Tracing signifying practices from slave narratives through Hurston and Reed, he argues that African American literature has \"its own internal genealogy\" governed by tropes of revision and double-voicedness, in which Black writers principally read and revise one another. From that he draws a positive prescription: criticism must theorize an African American canon as a self-interpreting tradition, \"not only refigure the white one.\" Choice B captures exactly this two-part move — Morrison's rereading claim is endorsed; the added contribution is that the African American tradition must itself be theorized as a self-interpreting genealogy, rather than treating rereading the dominant canon as the whole task.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice D: surface match — B uses Text 1's vocabulary (Poe, Cather, Hemingway, the Africanist figure) to recast Gates as rejecting Morrison's underlying close readings. Text 2 does the opposite: it explicitly accepts that the central works \"demand the kind of rereading she describes.\" His objection is what Morrison's project leaves out, not what it claims.\n- Choice C: scope shift — C reframes Gates's contribution as a methodological objection to rereading itself, in favor of a more conventional recovery-of-forgotten-authors program. Text 2 says the opposite of recovery-as-mere-addition: it agrees with Morrison that adding names is insufficient and proposes a theoretical reorientation, not a return to syllabus expansion.\n- Choice A: inverse — D inverts Gates's actual position by claiming that the African American tradition has \"already settled\" the questions Morrison raises, so further rereading of the white canon is unproductive. Text 2 frames the two projects as complementary (\"not only refigure the white one\"), not as one that retires the other. D treats a positive supplement as a categorical replacement that Gates explicitly avoids.",
