          "passage": "Even when no species has yet gone extinct, the gradual loss of animal populations from an ecosystem can quietly reshape the plants that remain. Conservation biologist Rodolfo Dirzo of Stanford University has spent decades documenting this process in Mexican tropical forests, and in a widely cited 2014 article he and his collaborators introduced the term defaunation to describe it. Drawing on long-term monitoring of large vertebrates such as tapirs, peccaries, and spider monkeys, Dirzo argues that the steady decline of these animals disrupts seed dispersal, trims plant reproduction, and slowly reorganizes ecosystem function. In his account, defaunation is therefore not a precursor to ecological harm but a form of ecological harm already underway, one whose effects are visible in the forest long before any species formally disappears.",
