          "passage": "Stanford social psychologist Claude M. Steele has spent much of his career studying why students from groups stereotyped as weaker in a given academic domain often perform below their measured ability on standardized tests. In a series of laboratory experiments first reported in the 1990s and synthesized in his 2010 book Whistling Vivaldi, Steele and his collaborators showed that even brief reminders of a negative stereotype, such as instructions framing a math test as one that produces gender differences, can measurably reduce the performance of students who belong to the targeted group. Steele uses the term stereotype threat to name this effect, and he argues that it operates as an additional cognitive load on test takers rather than as a reflection of underlying ability. On this account, the gap such tests appear to reveal is partly produced by the testing situation itself.",
