          "passage": "Anthropologist Nancy Scheper-Hughes spent more than two decades conducting fieldwork in a sugar-plantation shantytown in northeastern Brazil, where infant mortality was unusually high. Earlier theorists had assumed that grief over the death of an infant was a more or less universal response, varying only in surface expression. In her 1992 book Death Without Weeping, Scheper-Hughes describes mothers who, faced with chronic poverty and limited medical resources, withheld attachment from infants whose survival seemed unlikely and reserved fuller mourning for children who lived past the most precarious months. Rather than treating this restraint as emotional flatness, she argues that it is a culturally patterned adaptation to structural deprivation, an everyday response to economic violence that earlier universalist accounts of grief had failed to capture.",
