          "passage": "Beginning in the early 1980s, the artist Faith Ringgold composed pieced-fabric works she called story quilts, in which painted narrative panels are bordered by quilted cloth and inscribed with handwritten text. Ringgold's choice of medium was deliberate: in her 1995 memoir she observes that the quilt belonged to a long African American domestic tradition that museums had historically classified as craft rather than as fine art, and that working in quilted textile rather than oil on canvas allowed Black women's storytelling to be rooted in a practice the museum hierarchies had already excluded. The hierarchies, in other words, had treated medium as a marker of value, so that the same narrative ambition counted as a fine-art achievement on a stretched canvas and as a craft object when stitched in cloth. Ringgold's account therefore implies that ______",
