          "skill": "command-of-evidence-textual",
          "passage": "In her 1997 book The Spirit Catches You and You Fall Down: A Hmong Child, Her American Doctors, and the Collision of Two Cultures, the writer Anne Fadiman reconstructs the medical case of a young Hmong patient with severe epilepsy who was treated by physicians in northern California. Earlier accounts of similar cases, in her view, had treated the family's repeated departures from prescribed treatment as a problem of compliance, to be solved by clearer instructions and better interpreters. Fadiman rejects that diagnosis as a misreading of the situation. *She argues that what is recorded in such cases as noncompliance is in fact the visible surface of a deeper mismatch: family and clinicians are operating from incompatible models of what illness is, so that what looks like a refusal of treatment is really the continued pursuit, on each side, of a different and internally coherent course of action.*",
