          "explanation": "The correct answer is D. The underlined claim has two specific parts: (1) what is recorded as noncompliance is in fact the visible surface of a deeper mismatch in models of illness, and (2) what looks like refusal of treatment is really the continued, internally coherent pursuit of a different course of action on each side. Choice D maps onto both parts directly. It reframes the chart's label of \"noncompliance\" as \"the orderly continuation of a different treatment altogether\" (the each-side-pursuing-its-own-coherent-course side of the claim), and it locates the cause in the fact that \"the family and the physicians were not, at any point, addressing the same illness\" (the incompatible-models side of the claim). That is a direct, scoped match in Fadiman's register.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice C: surface match — the quotation uses Fadiman's documentary vocabulary (the staff, the family, the records, dosage and timing) and sounds like a passage from her case study. But its actual content locates the failure in a problem of translation — interpreters whose Hmong was \"uneven\" rendering crucial words in \"approximations\" — which is precisely the diagnosis the underlined sentence says she rejects. Better interpretation would, on this account, fix the problem; the claim is that the trouble lies below the level a translator could reach.\n- Choice A: scope shift — this is on-topic in that it concerns a specific clinical exchange in the case, but its scope is far narrower than the claim demands. It records a single afternoon visit, a single resident, and one missed dose. The claim is a general statement about how cross-cultural medical encounters get categorized as noncompliance when something deeper is going on; one anecdote about a satisfied resident illustrates a specific moment of miscommunication, not the structural mismatch the claim asserts.\n- Choice B: inverse — this quotation actually weakens the claim. It treats the lesson of the Hmong family's case as a recommendation for \"plainer instructions and steadier follow-up\" and assures the reader that \"most refusals dissolve once the schedule is properly explained.\" Choosing D would have Fadiman endorsing exactly the compliance-and-better-instructions diagnosis that the underlined sentence says she rejects.",
