          "skill": "command-of-evidence-textual",
          "passage": "In a series of essays on what is sometimes called the replication crisis in psychology, the social psychologist Brian Nosek, a co-founder of the Center for Open Science, set out to redirect a public conversation that he thought was running off course. Many readers, encountering reports that large multi-laboratory projects were recovering published effects at much lower rates than the original papers suggested, had drawn the conclusion that the field as a whole could not be trusted. Nosek rejects that as the wrong moral to draw. *He argues that the right response to inflated false-positive rates in the published literature is not a generalized loss of confidence in psychological science but the adoption of concrete reforms — preregistration of analyses, registered reports, and routine sharing of data and materials — that change the conditions under which research is produced and so address the structural source of the problem.*",
          "question": "Which quotation from Nosek's writing on the replication crisis most effectively illustrates the claim?",
