            {
              "id": "A",
              "text": "\"In one well-known study of an ego-depletion paradigm, twenty-three independent laboratories using a single shared protocol recovered an effect close to zero, whereas the original publication had reported a substantial decline in self-control between the two conditions.\""
            },
            {
              "id": "B",
              "text": "\"The pattern documented by these multi-lab projects is, on its face, a verdict on the discipline: a body of work that was produced under conditions inconsistent with reliable inference cannot be repaired piecemeal, and the rational response is to set most of it aside and to treat the published record with general skepticism until a new, more trustworthy literature has been built from the ground up.\""
            },
            {
              "id": "C",
              "text": "\"It is now apparent that the inflation of published effect sizes was always more modest than the most dramatic accounts suggested, and that once the noise introduced by a few non-representative laboratories is set aside, the great majority of classic psychological findings hold up under reasonable replication.\""
            },
            {
              "id": "D",
              "text": "\"The lesson the field should draw from these replication results is not that psychological science cannot be believed, but that the practices which produced an inflated published literature can themselves be changed: preregistered analyses remove most of the after-the-fact flexibility, registered reports neutralize the bias toward positive findings, and routine open data make the work checkable in a way it was not before.\""
            }
