          "explanation": "The correct answer is D. The underlined claim has two specific parts: (1) the right response to inflated false-positive rates is not a generalized loss of confidence in psychological science, and (2) it is instead the adoption of concrete reforms — preregistration, registered reports, and open data — that change the conditions under which research is produced. Choice D maps onto both parts directly. It opens by stating that \"the lesson the field should draw from these replication results is not that psychological science cannot be believed\" (the not-a-loss-of-confidence side of the claim), and it then names the very reforms the claim names: \"preregistered analyses remove most of the after-the-fact flexibility, registered reports neutralize the bias toward positive findings, and routine open data make the work checkable\" (the structural-reforms side). That is a direct, scoped match in Nosek's register.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice C: surface match — the quotation uses the relevant vocabulary (\"inflation of published effect sizes,\" \"replication,\" \"classic psychological findings\") and sounds like a defense of the field against alarmist coverage. But its actual content denies the diagnosis on which the claim rests: that there really is an inflated false-positive rate that needs addressing. The claim accepts that inflation as genuine and proposes structural reforms to correct it; Choice C treats the inflation as overstated and locates the trouble in \"a few non-representative laboratories,\" which is a different argument.\n- Choice A: scope shift — this is on-topic in that it reports a multi-lab replication result, but its scope is far narrower than the claim demands. It describes a single ego-depletion study and a single failed replication of one effect. The claim is a general statement about how the field should respond to the broader pattern of inflated false positives; one ego-depletion result is consistent with the claim but does not, on its own, illustrate the program of structural reform the underlined sentence asserts.\n- Choice B: inverse — this quotation actually weakens the claim. It draws from the same replication evidence the conclusion the claim explicitly rejects: that the published literature \"cannot be repaired piecemeal,\" that the rational response is \"to set most of it aside,\" and to treat the record with \"general skepticism\" until a new literature is built from the ground up. Choosing D would have Nosek endorsing exactly the generalized-loss-of-confidence response that the underlined sentence says he opposes.",
