          "explanation": "The correct answer is C, \"control. It\". The blank sits between two complete independent clauses. The first clause — \"The book did not call for a blanket ban on chemical pest control\" — has its own subject (\"The book\") and verb (\"did not call\") and could stand alone. The second clause — \"it argued, instead, that compounds shown to accumulate in the tissues of birds and other predators should be removed from agricultural use until their long-term ecological effects had been more carefully studied\" — has its own subject (\"it\") and verb (\"argued\") and could also stand alone. Standard English requires either a period, a semicolon, or a comma plus a coordinating conjunction between two independent clauses. Choice C supplies a period and capitalizes the next word, which is the cleanest way to close the first clause and open the second.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B (\"control, it\"): surface match — this is the classic comma splice. A comma between two independent clauses without a coordinating conjunction is not licensed in Standard English; the comma can sit there only if a conjunction such as \"and\" or \"but\" follows.\n- Choice D (\"control it\"): a different SEC error — supplying no punctuation at all between the two complete clauses produces a fused (run-on) sentence in which the reader cannot tell where the first clause ends and the second begins.\n- Choice A (\"control and, it\"): inverse — D inserts \"and,\" punctuated as if \"it\" were a parenthetical. \"And\" can join independent clauses only when it is preceded by a comma and followed directly by the next clause; placing the comma after \"and\" rather than before it breaks the conjunction off from its subject and produces a sentence that does not parse.",
