          "explanation": "The correct answer is D, 'Manzanilla showed.' The sentence opens with the participial phrase 'Excavating the residential cores room by room,' which must modify the subject of the main clause. Standard English requires the doer of the participial action — the person who was excavating — to be the grammatical subject of the clause that follows. Manzanilla was the one doing the excavation, so she must be the subject of the main clause. Choice D places 'Manzanilla' in subject position, so the modifier attaches correctly: 'Excavating the residential cores room by room, Manzanilla showed that craft specialization …'\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- (C) 'the evidence showed Manzanilla' makes 'the evidence' the subject of the main clause, which produces a dangling modifier: evidence cannot 'excavate the residential cores room by room.' This is the surface-match distractor — it keeps Manzanilla in the sentence but in object position, so the participial phrase has no proper noun to attach to.\n- (A) 'it was shown by Manzanilla' makes the dummy 'it' the grammatical subject, which again leaves the participle dangling: 'it' cannot have done the excavating. This is the inverse choice on subject-of-modifier: the doer is demoted to a by-phrase, and the subject slot is filled by an expletive.\n- (B) 'there was evidence shown' commits a different SEC error (existential 'there' construction): the grammatical subject is the existential 'there,' and the participle 'Excavating' has nothing to attach to. As with (C) and (A), the only way to repair the sentence is to make the actual excavator — Manzanilla — the subject of the main clause.",
