          "explanation": "The first sentence describes the conventional seafloor model and the surveys built on it: depth, substrate type, and temperature are treated as the dominant axes of species distribution. The second sentence presents Levin's contrasting empirical finding — species whose distributions track dissolved-oxygen concentration more tightly than any conventional axis, including species absent from waters that look identical on temperature and substrate alone. The relation between the two sentences is a direct A-versus-B opposition: the conventional model on one side, Levin's oxygen-driven counter-evidence on the other. \"By contrast,\" is the CB-lexicon transition for that paired-opposition move and is correct (C).\n\nThe key cue that selects \"By contrast,\" rather than the more generic \"However,\" is the parallel structure: sentence 1 names a model (depth/substrate/temperature), and sentence 2 names a competing axis (dissolved oxygen) explicitly tested against the same conventional axes (\"identical on temperature and substrate alone\"). \"By contrast,\" is built precisely for that direct A-vs-B juxtaposition between two named, parallel claims.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice D (\"Likewise,\"): inverse — \"Likewise,\" signals continuation by parallel: sentence 2 would be doing the same thing as sentence 1 on a different case. But Levin's finding does the opposite: it cuts against the conventional model by showing species that do not track the conventional axes. \"Likewise,\" would fit if sentence 2 supplied additional evidence for depth/substrate/temperature; instead it supplies counter-evidence.\n- Choice A (\"For instance,\"): scope shift — \"For instance,\" signals that sentence 2 illustrates a general claim from sentence 1. But Levin's species are not an example of the conventional depth-substrate-temperature framework; they are the case the conventional framework cannot accommodate. Reading sentence 2 as an illustration of sentence 1 misidentifies a counter-example as a confirming instance.\n- Choice B (\"Consequently,\"): surface match — \"Consequently,\" signals that sentence 2 follows as a logical consequence of sentence 1. The strongest distractor here, because the existence of conventional surveys is part of the same scientific conversation as Levin's critique. But Levin's empirical finding is not derived from the conventional model as its consequence; it is offered against the model as a competing pattern. The relation is opposition between two claims, not entailment from one to the other.",
