          "explanation": "The correct answer is D, \"register.\" The first sentence establishes that bone collagen \"preserves a chemical signature\" of the last decade of a person's diet, and the second sentence asks how those signatures relate to dietary change in medieval populations. The verb in the blank therefore needs to mean \"to record or take in faithfully.\" \"Register\" fits exactly: it captures the idea that the isotope values neutrally pick up and hold a record of dietary shifts, without implying that the bones themselves remember or distort the information.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B: scope shift — \"exaggerate\" has the right general direction (the isotopes do reflect diet), but the wrong specificity and connotation. The passage describes isotope values as a chemical signature that tracks dietary shifts; nothing in the text suggests they amplify or overstate those shifts. \"Exaggerate\" pushes a neutral act of recording into an act of distortion that the sentence does not claim.\n- Choice C: inverse — \"obscure\" reverses the central claim of the paragraph. The point of citing Reitsema's work is precisely that bone-collagen ratios let researchers reconstruct diet even when written sources are missing; \"obscure\" would mean the signatures hide the very dietary information the sentence credits them with revealing.\n- Choice A: surface match — \"memorize\" plays on the verbs \"preserve\" and \"record\" earlier in the passage, so its general flavor of holding information feels topically right. But \"memorize\" implies a conscious agent committing material to memory; isotope ratios are inert chemical traces in bone, not a mind that learns. The collocation pulls a reader toward the wrong kind of preservation.",
