          "passage": "In *Death Without Weeping* (1992), the anthropologist Nancy Scheper-Hughes describes the muted public response to infant death in a poor northeastern Brazilian shantytown where as many as half of all children failed to reach their first birthday. Earlier theorists had read such restraint as evidence of a universal human stance toward bereavement. Scheper-Hughes rejects that reading. The flattened affect she observed, she argues, is better understood as ______ the chronic structural deprivation under which mothers were trying to raise children — a learned protection against attachments that the local infant mortality rate made unsustainable.",
