          "correctAnswer": "D",
          "explanation": "The correct answer is D. The poem is built from three stanzas, each opened by the same first-person claim of felt knowledge. Stanza 1 begins \"I know what the caged bird feels\" and develops that knowledge as a longing for the spring world outside the cage — the bright sun on the upland slopes, the wind through the springing grass, the river like a stream of glass, the first bird's song and the first opening bud. Stanza 2 begins \"I know why the caged bird beats his wing\" and develops that knowledge as the bird's struggle: he beats his wing till the blood reddens the bars, must return to his perch when he would be on the bough, and feels a pain that throbs again in the old scars. Stanza 3 begins \"I know why the caged bird sings\" and develops that knowledge as the bird's song, which the speaker explicitly reframes: it is \"not a carol of joy or glee\" but \"a prayer\" and \"a plea\" sent upward from the heart's deep core. The structure is therefore a triple claim of felt knowledge — longing, struggle, song — with the third stanza reinterpreting what looks like song into plea. Choice D captures all three motions and the reframing in the third.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B: inverse — A reverses the speaker's stance. The poem is built from declarations (\"I know what the caged bird feels,\" \"I know why he beats his wing,\" \"I know why the caged bird sings\"), not from questions; and there is no outside authority to whom the speaker defers. The speaker is the authority; the structure is a triple assertion of his knowledge, the opposite of a series of unanswered questions.\n- Choice C: surface match — B picks up the bright spring imagery in stanza 1 and treats it as a portrait of a separate, free bird that is then contrasted with the caged one. The first stanza is not about a free bird at all; the spring world is what the caged bird longs for from within the cage, and the conclusion the poem actually reaches is not that two birds are alike but that the caged bird's song is a plea, not a song of joy.\n- Choice A: scope shift — D imports a remembered childhood encounter and an argument against the practice of caging birds, neither of which the poem contains. The speaker offers no anecdote about a particular bird he once saw and no policy claim about whether wild birds should be kept in cages; the poem operates entirely as a sustained interior identification with the caged bird's experience.",
