          "skill": "text-structure-and-purpose",
          "passage": "The following text is adapted from a speech by the suffragist Lucy Stone, delivered in 1853 at a hearing of the Massachusetts Constitutional Convention. Stone is addressing the delegates directly on the question of whether women should be entitled to vote in the new state constitution.\n\nGentlemen of the Convention, I do not come before you to ask any new principle, nor to plead a doctrine of my own invention. I come, rather, to ask the application of a principle which you have already declared to be the corner-stone of this commonwealth: that government derives its just powers from the consent of the governed. Half of those whom you propose to govern by the constitution now in your hands have given no such consent, and have not been asked for it. They are taxed in their property, tried in your courts, and punished by your laws, and in all of this they are governed; but in the choice of those who shall make the laws, they have no voice whatever. I would put the matter to you very simply. Either the principle of consent is binding here, in which case its application cannot stop at one half of the people; or it is not, in which case the foundation you have laid for this commonwealth is open to question. I ask you to choose.",
