          "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Stone opens by saying explicitly that she has not come to ask the delegates to adopt any new principle but to ask \"the application of a principle which you have already declared to be the corner-stone of this commonwealth\" — namely, that \"government derives its just powers from the consent of the governed.\" She then shows that the principle, as currently applied, leaves out half of those who will be governed: women are taxed, tried in the courts, and punished by the laws, but have \"no voice whatever\" in choosing the lawmakers. Her closing move is to lay the consequence at the delegates' feet as a binary: either the consent principle binds, in which case it cannot stop at one half of the people, or it does not, in which case the foundation of the commonwealth is open to question — \"I ask you to choose.\" The text's purpose is therefore to argue that the delegates' own principle, if binding, must extend to women, and to force the delegates to face the choice their own foundation requires. Choice A captures both halves of that purpose at the right level of generalization.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B: scope shift — the speech does name several disabilities (taxation, courts, punishment by laws), so a hurried reader can mistake the catalogue for the purpose. But Stone uses these disabilities as evidence that women are governed without their consent; they are a subordinate move within a larger argument about a principle, not the subject of the speech. Treating them as the purpose narrows the speech from a constitutional argument to a list of grievances.\n- Choice D: surface match — Stone speaks in the first person (\"I do not come before you...,\" \"I would put the matter to you very simply\"), which can lead a hurried reader to read the speech as a personal narrative. But the first person is rhetorical scaffolding for the public argument; she gives no account of how she came to the suffrage movement, and she asks the delegates not to take an interest in her experience but to apply their own stated principle.\n- Choice C: inverse — D reverses Stone's actual position. She argues precisely that the consent-of-the-governed principle, if it binds at all, must be applied to women; D has her counseling the delegates to keep the principle as an abstract ideal but to withhold it from women under the new constitution. Choosing D requires reading the speech in the opposite direction from the binary Stone explicitly poses (\"either the principle of consent is binding here ... or it is not\").",
