              "label": "Text 1",
              "text": "The nutrition epidemiologist Walter Willett, drawing on decades of cohort data from the Nurses' Health Study and parallel cohorts, has argued that the type of fats and carbohydrates a population habitually consumes materially affects long-term cardiovascular and metabolic risk, beyond the question of caloric balance alone. Replacing saturated with unsaturated fats, and refined carbohydrates with whole grains, is associated in those data with substantially lower coronary disease incidence over follow-up periods of twenty years and more. The implication, on his account, is that public dietary guidance should not stop at energy intake but should be specific about food quality: which fats, which carbohydrates, and in what relative proportions. Cohort epidemiology, he maintains, has now produced the evidence base such recommendations require."
