              "text": "The methodologist John Ioannidis, surveying the broader nutritional epidemiology literature, accepts that diet matters for chronic disease and does not contest the general direction of the cohort findings on fats and grains. His concern lies one level upstream, in the methods that generate the specific effect sizes recommendations rely on. Self-reported food-frequency questionnaires, he notes, have substantial measurement error; the analytical menu of nutrients, time windows, and adjustment sets is large; and the literature exhibits a strong tendency for large initial associations to attenuate or fail to replicate. Across thousands of nutrient-disease associations he has examined, this pattern is the rule rather than the exception. The case for confident, food-specific guidance, he therefore argues, is weaker than the cohort summaries can make it appear."
