          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "Bacteria have long been known to defend themselves against viruses through a system of repeated DNA segments called CRISPR, but until 2012 the molecular machinery underlying that defense remained only partly understood. In a paper published that year, microbiologist Emmanuelle Charpentier and her collaborators, including biochemist Jennifer Doudna, demonstrated that the bacterial Cas9 protein cleaves invading DNA only when it is paired with two short guide RNAs derived from the CRISPR system. By fusing those two natural guides into a single engineered RNA, the researchers showed that Cas9 can be reprogrammed to cut essentially any chosen DNA sequence in a test tube. From this experimental result they argued that the bacterial CRISPR system, originally an immune defense, could be repurposed as a general-purpose gene-editing tool, a claim that quickly reshaped molecular biology.",
