          "passage": "Cultural critic bell hooks spent much of her career writing about the conditions under which classrooms can become genuinely transformative. In her 1994 book Teaching to Transgress, hooks rejects what she calls the banking model of education, in which an authoritative teacher deposits content into passive students. She argues that this model leaves the political and emotional lives of students outside the classroom door and so reproduces, rather than disrupts, existing hierarchies. Drawing on her own teaching at predominantly white universities, hooks proposes an alternative she calls engaged pedagogy, in which teachers treat students as already knowing subjects and themselves as fellow learners. On this account, education becomes a practice of freedom only when classrooms admit the full social experience of the people in them.",
