          "explanation": "The correct answer is B. The passage develops its main idea by setting up a contrast and then drawing a conclusion. Sentence 2 names the model hooks rejects — banking education. Sentence 3 explains why she rejects it: it leaves students' political and emotional lives outside the classroom and so reproduces hierarchies. Sentence 4 introduces her positive alternative, engaged pedagogy, in which teachers and students are fellow learners. Sentence 5 states the broader claim that education becomes a practice of freedom only when classrooms admit students' full social experience. Choice B captures both halves of the contrast and the conditional conclusion at the right level of generalization.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A: scope shift — restates the rejected half of the contrast (the banking model and its problems) but omits the positive proposal of engaged pedagogy and the conditional claim about education as a practice of freedom. The passage develops both halves of the argument; treating only the critique as the main idea narrows the passage's scope.\n- Choice D: surface match — restates a biographical detail (her teaching at predominantly white universities, the title Teaching to Transgress) without naming either the rejected model or the alternative she proposes. A description of where the argument was developed is not the argument itself.\n- Choice C: inverse — reverses the passage's central claim. hooks rejects the banking model and its reliance on authoritative teaching; choice C has her defending that model as necessary for freedom, contradicting the main idea.",
