          "passage": "Across many fields, calls to decolonize curricula, methods, and institutions have multiplied since the early 2000s, often as shorthand for a wide range of projects aimed at greater equity and inclusion. In a widely read 2012 essay, education scholar Eve Tuck and her co-author K. Wayne Yang grant that this broader usage has expanded what counts as legitimate scholarly engagement. They argue, however, that when decolonization is treated as a generic figure for institutional reform — for diversifying syllabi, revising research methods, or rethinking citation practices — its specific historical meaning is hollowed out. For Tuck, decolonization remains, at its core, a material project bound to the return of land and political sovereignty to Indigenous peoples; the broader reform projects, she suggests, are valuable on their own terms but should not be described in language that displaces that material claim.",
