          "passage": "Standard textbook accounts have long credited the painters Wassily Kandinsky and Piet Mondrian, both working in the early 1910s, with the first fully nonrepresentational paintings in European art. The Swedish artist Hilma af Klint, who exhibited her work mainly within a small esoteric circle in Stockholm rather than through the avant-garde galleries of Paris, Munich, or Moscow, painted large-scale nonrepresentational canvases as early as 1906. Curator Iris Müller-Westermann, who organized a major retrospective of af Klint's work at the Moderna Museet, has noted that the dating of these canvases is supported by inscriptions and notebooks contemporaneous with the paintings themselves. Müller-Westermann's findings therefore imply that the textbook chronology of European abstract painting ______",
