          "passage": "Aboriginal Australian art historian Hetti Perkins has argued that paintings from desert communities in central Australia should not be approached primarily as decorative regional ornament adapted to Western canvas. Such paintings, she observes in her 2010 book Art + Soul, are organized around the artist's relationship to a specific Country—a particular tract of land whose features carry inherited legal and cosmological obligations—and the marks on the surface encode knowledge about that Country which the artist is authorized to transmit. Whether a viewer reads a given mark as figure or as ground, Perkins notes, is therefore secondary; what is decisive is whether the artist holds the right to record that knowledge in the first place. Perkins's account therefore implies that an account that evaluates such a painting solely by its formal visual qualities ______",
