          "passage": "In curatorial work on bark paintings from Arnhem Land, in northern Australia, Margie West has stressed an aspect of the form that early ethnographic catalogues often missed. The figural images on these barks, West observes in her 1988 book The Inspired Dream, encode ancestral narratives whose interpretation is layered for different levels of initiation: features that one viewer reads as a generic depiction of an animal or a watering place may carry, for an initiated viewer, restricted information about specific ancestral events tied to particular tracts of Country. The painted surface is the same in both cases; what differs is how much of the encoded narrative the viewer is authorized to read. West's analysis therefore implies that a museum catalogue that records only the surface description of such a painting ______",
