          "passage": "In her 2021 book Finding the Mother Tree: Discovering the Wisdom of the Forest, the forest ecologist Suzanne Simard reflects on three decades of isotope-tracing experiments in the Douglas-fir forests of British Columbia. Earlier readers of her work, she observes, have often taken her measured carbon transfers between trees as evidence that older trees deliberately tend to younger ones, almost as parents do. Simard sets out to refine that picture. *She argues that the cooperative-looking outcome at the level of the forest does not require any tree-level intention; the carbon flows she has measured are the predictable consequence of a physical-chemical process — sugar moves down concentration gradients within shared fungal mycelia — and the apparent care of older trees for younger ones is what that process looks like when viewed at the scale of the stand.*",
