          "skill": "command-of-evidence-textual",
          "passage": "In her 1981 book Symbiosis in Cell Evolution, the evolutionary biologist Lynn Margulis pressed her account of the origin of complex cells into a wider claim about evolution itself. Earlier in her career, she had argued — in the 1967 paper \"On the Origin of Mitosing Cells\" — that mitochondria and chloroplasts had begun as free-living bacteria engulfed by ancestral host cells. Critics had treated that proposal as a one-off curiosity even after the molecular evidence began to support it. Margulis rejected the curiosity framing. *She argues that long-term symbiotic mergers between distantly related organisms are not isolated oddities but a recurring engine of evolutionary innovation in their own right, capable of producing in a single inheritable step the kind of structural novelty that the standard picture, which relies on the steady accumulation of small competitive advantages within a single lineage, can only build up over many generations.*",
