          "explanation": "The correct answer is D. The underlined claim has two specific parts: (1) long-term symbiotic mergers between distantly related organisms are not isolated oddities but a recurring engine of evolutionary innovation in their own right, and (2) such mergers can produce, in a single inheritable step, the kind of structural novelty that the standard small-advantage picture can only build up over many generations. Choice D maps onto both parts directly. It denies that symbiotic merger is \"the curiosity it has been treated as\" and identifies it instead as \"a recurring source of evolutionary novelty in its own right\" (the recurring-engine side of the claim), and it then specifies that such a partnership can deliver \"in a single inheritable step\" the kind of innovation that \"the gradualist picture of accumulated small advantages within one lineage requires hundreds of generations to assemble\" (the contrast-with-the-standard-picture side). That is a direct, scoped match in Margulis's register.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A: surface match — the quotation uses Margulis's molecular-evidence vocabulary (mitochondria and chloroplasts, circular DNA, ribosomes, inner membranes resembling bacteria) and sounds like a recognizable sentence from her work. But its actual content is a confirmation of the original 1967 organelle-features hypothesis. The claim in the underlined sentence builds outward from that case to a general thesis about symbiotic merger as a recurring engine of evolutionary innovation; Choice A stays inside the original mitochondria-and-chloroplasts case and does not make the wider claim the sentence asserts.\n- Choice C: scope shift — this is on-topic in that it concerns symbiotic arrangements and would fit comfortably in a Margulis chapter, but its scope is far narrower than the claim demands. It describes one afternoon in one salt marsh and a dozen ciliates with photosynthetic partners, and it goes only as far as calling the variety \"suggestive.\" The claim is a general statement about symbiotic merger as a recurring engine of evolutionary innovation; one observational anecdote illustrates the existence of present-day partnerships, not the generative role across evolutionary history that the underlined sentence asserts.\n- Choice B: inverse — this quotation actually weakens the claim. It treats the larger architecture of evolutionary change as \"the slow accumulation of small competitive advantages within continuing lineages\" and frames merger episodes as \"exceptions to that picture rather than as a parallel engine working alongside it.\" Choosing D would have Margulis endorsing exactly the gradualist-architecture-with-merger-as-exception view that the underlined sentence says she rejects.",
