          "explanation": "The correct answer is A, \"population; however,\". The blank sits at the junction of two complete independent clauses linked by the conjunctive adverb \"however.\" The first clause runs from \"Earlier syntheses\" through \"a generic urban population,\" and the second runs from \"in the multifamily compound of Teopancazco\" through \"co-residents of a single compound\"; each clause has its own subject and finite verb and could stand alone as a sentence. When two independent clauses are joined and the second is introduced by a conjunctive adverb such as however, therefore, instead, or rather, Standard English requires a semicolon (or a period) at the actual clause boundary, with a comma after the conjunctive adverb. Choice A places the semicolon where the boundary actually is — after \"population\" — and the comma after \"however,\" producing the canonical \"…population; however, in the multifamily compound…\" pattern.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B (\"population, however,\"): surface match — paired commas around \"however\" make the option resemble a parenthetical interrupter inserted inside a single clause (the way one might bracket a brief aside such as \", however,\" in mid-sentence). Here, though, \"however\" is not embedded inside one clause; it links two complete independent clauses. A comma alone between two independent clauses produces a comma splice, regardless of whether \"however\" is set off by another comma. Conjunctive adverbs do not have the joining power of FANBOYS coordinating conjunctions.\n- Choice C (\"population, however;\"): scope shift — the option flips the punctuation, putting a comma where the actual clause boundary lies (after \"population\") and a semicolon after \"however.\" The semicolon then falls inside the second clause rather than between the two clauses, so it neither joins the independent clauses nor separates anything that needs separating. The result is a comma splice at the real boundary plus a stray semicolon mid-clause.\n- Choice D (\"population however,\"): inverse — the opposite of the correct boundary treatment. With no punctuation at all between \"population\" and \"however,\" the two independent clauses are fused into a run-on; the comma after \"however\" cannot retroactively supply the missing clause boundary. Standard English requires a semicolon or period at the junction of two independent clauses linked by a conjunctive adverb.",
